  "notes": "Jive male soloist in late-90s and 2000s adult R&B, with Teddy Riley supplying his biggest uptempo, 1997–2004. The research gives one hard datum — a \"66 ballad spine (backbeat on 3)\" — and marks the 90–100 uptempo band `[LOW-CONF]`, so the ballad spine is what is encoded: 62–70 felt, `halfTime` true, `halftime_3` snare. Three drum deltas: \"kick on 1\" at one or two hits a bar, \"brushed hats\" as an 8th lane at 0.09 fill density, and — the distinguishing one — Riley's \"stuttered gated snare figure as the hook device\", carried as a `snareRoll` block on 32nds with `strong_weak_weak_weak` grouping and a 0.35 gap probability, which nothing else in this batch authors. Harmony is \"extension-rich minor vamps with a piano/Rhodes lead\", so seventh and ninth carry 0.86 between them over two- and three-chord families. \"Smooth mid-tenor with controlled melisma, harmony in close thirds, note-density moderate\" is the mid register, the 3–7 density and the narrow interval spread. ⚠ Confidence is **low**, and the research says exactly why: \"one sourced tempo point and one sourced producer credit; much of the grammar is board-level\", with the key claim and the bridge modulation both marked `[LOW-CONF]`. The entry is kept because \"the Riley link is a documented, load-bearing lane anchor\". ⚠ The band is INFERRED around a single point: the entry gives \"66 ballad spine (backbeat on 3)\" and no ballad band, so the 62–70 cushion is this author's and only the mode, 66, is a reading; the entry's separate \"uptempos in the 90–100 straight band\" are a different backbeat class and are not folded in. ⚠ Every velocity, density and register number, and the whole key weighting, is INFERRED.",
